          "derivation": "The box packs two words of very different date. (a) رطل raṭl / riṭl 'a weight of twelve ounces; a large vessel for drink' really is Greek λίτρα, but it reached Arabic through Aramaic, not directly: Gk λίτρα → Jewish Palestinian Aramaic / Classical Syriac līṭrā → Aramaic rīṭlā (dissimilation of the first l to r before the second liquid, with metathesis of the vowel) → Arabic raṭl/riṭl; Greek τ is regularly rendered by the emphatic ط in Aramaic-mediated Greek loans. Lokotsch no. 1708 states it flatly: 'Ar. raṭl: „Ein Gewicht“ [aus gr. λίτρα; ZDMG LI, 298; de Lagarde, Gesammelte Abhandlungen 33]', adding Fleischer's point that the Arabs themselves felt riṭl to be the better pronunciation, ratl prevailing under the influence of the first two consonants. Nourai's NOTE, '„ratl“ is a distorted form of „lîtr“', is therefore substantially right, though the 'distortion' is a regular Aramaic development rather than a corruption inside Arabic; and the intermediate Aramaic stage is missing from the chart. (b) Arabic لتر/ليتر lītr 'liter' is not this word at all: it is a nineteenth-century loan from French litre taken with the metric system, so putting it under Ancient Greek λίτρα is wrong — it belongs with node #4 under the French box. Worst verdict therefore governs: plausible.",
