          "derivation": "All three are learned Latinisms, borrowed in writing, not inherited. Libra is Latin lībra taken over unchanged as the name of the zodiacal Scales (in English astronomical use from Middle English on). deliberate is the Latin participle dēlīberātus, adjective from early 15c. ('done with careful consideration'), verb from the 1540s ('weigh in the mind'), with the sense 'consciously unhurried' from the 1590s. equilibrium is Latin aequilibrium 'even balance', aequus 'equal' + lībra. Klein's libra entry (p.886) cross-refers exactly this set — 'Cp. librate, deliberate, equilibrium' — and Skeat s.v. equilibrium: 'L. aequilibris, evenly balanced. — L. aequi- for aequus, even; libra, to balance; see Librate.' The same Latin word also underlies the abbreviations lb. and £, and It. lira and Fr. livre, which AHD lists under the same etymon.",
